### Run PySpice Examples in Various Environments

Source: https://github.com/pyspice-org/pyspice/blob/master/doc/sphinx/source/example-introduction.rst

This section provides commands to execute PySpice examples using different methods: directly from the console, within the IPython interactive environment, and via IPython Notebook. Choose the method that best suits your workflow for editing or interactive experimentation.

```sh
python examples/.../foo.py
```

```sh
ipython --matplotlib       # enable matplotlib integration
ipython --matplotlib-qt    # enable matplotlib integration with qt4 backend
ipython qtconsole          # start the qtconsole GUI application
```

```py
%run examples/../foo.py
```

```sh
ipython notebook
```

```py
%matplotlib inline
```

--------------------------------

### Execute Dynamically Linked ngspice C Application

Source: https://github.com/pyspice-org/pyspice/blob/master/examples/c-examples/ngspice_cb/compile-linux.txt

This command executes the C application previously compiled for dynamic linking to the ngspice shared library. For successful execution, the ngspice shared library must be discoverable at runtime, typically by being in a standard library path or by setting the LD_LIBRARY_PATH environment variable. Ensure you are in the correct directory (e.g., ng_shared_test) before running.

```Shell
./bin/Debug/ng_sh
```

--------------------------------

### Statically Link ngspice Shared Library to C Application

Source: https://github.com/pyspice-org/pyspice/blob/master/examples/c-examples/ngspice_cb/compile-linux.txt

This command compiles a C source file (main.c) and statically links it against the ngspice shared library (-lngspice) and the OpenMP runtime library (-lgomp). The output is an executable named ng_sh in the ./bin/Debug directory. This method embeds the library code directly into the executable, making it self-contained.

```C
gcc -g -Wall -lgomp -lngspice -o ./bin/Debug/ng_sh main.c
```

--------------------------------

### Dynamically Link ngspice Shared Library to C Application at Runtime

Source: https://github.com/pyspice-org/pyspice/blob/master/examples/c-examples/ngspice_cb/compile-linux.txt

This command compiles a C source file (main.c) for dynamic linking against the ngspice shared library at runtime. It uses the dl library (-ldl) to handle dynamic loading and also links against gomp for OpenMP. The output is an executable named ng_sh, which will resolve library dependencies at runtime.

```C
gcc -Wall -g -lgomp -ldl -o ./bin/Debug/ng_sh main.c
```

